The change from `allSettled` to `Promise.all` + a hand-rolled `TerminalDisposalOutcome` union + try/catch + a 3-array partition loop only exists to recover which id failed. `allSettled` preserves input order, so the id is the index:

```ts
const ids = [...new Set(terminalIds)];
const results = await Promise.allSettled(
	ids.map(async (terminalId) => { /* body unchanged */ }),
);
const terminated = ids.filter((_, i) => results[i].status === "fulfilled");
const failed = ids.filter((_, i) => results[i].status === "rejected");
const errors = results.flatMap((r) => (r.status === "rejected" ? [r.reason] : []));
```

Drops the 3-line type, the try/catch nesting and the 11-line partition loop (~13 lines net), and restores the pre-change `errors` flatMap so the diff against the old shape is smaller. Behavior identical. Cost: ~10 min, low risk, existing tests cover both modes.

**2. `C:\Users\khair\Code Projects\fork-superset-windows-arm64\packages\host-service\src\companion\lifecycle-alerts.ts:1172-1182` — `retireWhere` builds `matched` with a manual loop.**

```ts
const matched = [...alerts.values()].filter(predicate);
for (const alert of matched) retireAlert(alert, reason);
return matched.length;
```

Same snapshot-then-retire semantics (materialized before any mutation), 4 lines → 1. Cost: ~2 min.

